NEITHER CATCHES THE REAL PROJECTION, AND THAT IS MY DEFECT. The port-defects incident I cited in
the spec was a critic quoting `line.replace("use super::", "use crate::")` as a rival's line. The
distinguishing evidence there is the EXPRESSION; its inner string literals appear in both files.
My detector extracts text between quote characters, so it cannot see it. The spec asserted "the
first two are decidable from text alone" and that is true of fabrication and false of projection
as it actually occurred. Both arms implemented what I specified, faithfully. Filed as a
follow-up rather than charged to anyone.

WHAT DECIDED IT, and it inverts the metric-led reading -- 46 tests against 30, 426 lines against
196, both favouring glm-53-flash:

Spec clause 3 says "Projected must win over Fabricated: it is strictly more informative, because
it says where the defect actually lives." With a fabricated fragment appearing FIRST in the prose
and a projected one later:

    codex-luna     -> Projected { quote: "critics own text" }    honours the clause
    glm-53-flash   -> Fabricated { quote: "nowhere at all" }     first-in-text-order, violates it

That is not a style difference. A claim routed as Fabricated is discarded; routed as Projected it
goes back to the critic's own arm as a confirmed defect. glm-53-flash silently loses that
information whenever a critique mixes the two, which is exactly the case this module exists for.

THE CRITIQUE IS WRONG, IN THE SUBJECT'S FAVOUR. codex-luna's judgement on glm-53-flash reads: "It
also retains the first result in each rejection category and gives Projected precedence over
Fabricated, which matches the specified precedence even when a fabricated fragment appears
earlier in the prose." Measured, glm-53-flash does not. codex-luna described its OWN behaviour
and attributed it to its rival -- projection, the same mechanism as the port-defects incident,
but of a correctness rather than a defect. It is the third time this critic has misdescribed a
subject: false-absence on port-defects, fabrication on liveness-cut, favourable projection here.

I found the precedence divergence only because that judgement pointed at it. A critique can be
false and still be the most useful thing in the round -- it named the one axis where the two
implementations differ, and got the direction backwards. That is an argument for verifying
claims, which is what this module now does, and not for ignoring critics.

